  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nA cold front moved across the area overnight and early this \nmorning. Behind the front, gusty northerly winds were noted as \ntemperatures ranged from the upper 30s far north to around 50 \nacross east-central WI at noon. Scattered rain showers were\nnoted across the north and across northeast WI. \n\nLatest model guidance suggest strong cold advection across\nnortheast Wisconsin this afternoon behind the front. Bufkit\nsoundings indicated steep low level lapse rates that will help\nmix down the stronger winds aloft. Went a little higher on wind \ngusts this afternoon based on the fact latest wind gusts across \ncentral and north-central Wisconsin were a little higher than \nnumerical guidance. Bufkit soundings still support the strongest \nwind gusts over northern Door County where gusts to around 40 mph\nare possible. Significant differences in the NBM and LREF probability\nguidance for winds of 40 mph greater over northern Door County. \nThe NBM indicated only a 20% chance of gusts over 40 mph while \nthe LREF was at 60-70% Looking at bufkit data at Deaths Door \n(northern Door County), soundings would support wind gust to or \naround 40 mph, especially between 2 pm and 7 pm. Meanwhile over \nthe north, precipitation is likely to change over to snow over \nnorth-central WI. A light dusting of snow will be possible, \nespecially across Vilas County. Of note, the latest HRRR indicated\nbands of snow moving into north-central and far northeast \nWisconsin during the night, thus there is a very small chance for \nlocalized higher snowfall amounts over a half inch if these bands \nmaterialize. Confidence is low if this would occur, and exactly \nwhere it would occur. Lastly, an upper level disturbance combined \nwith the northerly flow could bring a chance of rain showers to far\neastern Wisconsin later tonight into Friday morning, especially \nacross Door County. \n\nOtherwise, tranquil conditions are expected through the middle of\nnext week. After near normal temperatures on Saturday, temperatures\nshould run well above normal next week.",
